This is the last capstone challenge for the SOC 1 pathway! 

Situation: The threat actor Boogeyman was able to infilitrate the organization through another phishing email. I have access to logs through Elastic Stack (ELK).

We were also given an estimate of the date range, so that helped narrow down the amount of logs that had to be looked through. 

To begin, I tried to get more information about the initial payload. By looking up the name of the file in the logs, I was able to find the earliest event with a PID of 6392.

By looking at the other processes under the payload name, I saw that the attacker tried to copy the the file to another location as well. In another entry, it was shown to have successfully run with the rundll32 executable.

Some more information provided was that there was a persistance mechanism initiated by the payload, so I looked for rundll32 scheduled tasks. It was also shared that the file execution created a C2 connection. 

To find the IP address information for this, I queried for powershell in ELK. Once that was done, I backtracked again. The next task shared that the administrator access was used. By looking up the name of the scheduled task again, I was able to find the process the attacker used for privilege escalation.

